  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nUnsettled weather conditions return tomorrow as a low pressure \nsystem from the north slides into New York tomorrow morning. \nThis weather system brings additional beneficial rainfall to \neastern New York and western New York with medium (greater than \n50%) to high chances of rain showers throughout the day. At this\ntime, we are not expecting any flooding concerns as rainfall\nrates are forecasted to be less than 0.75 inches per hour with\nany heavier bands of rain and the location of higher rainfall\ntotals is in the southern Adirondacks to Mohawk Valley (totals\nbetween 0.5 inches and 1 inch is expected). Tuesday afternoon \ninto the early evening hours, there could be a few rumbles of \nthunder across locations north of Albany. For locations south \nof Albany, confidence is low for rumbles of thunder as \ningredients are not strongly favored to materialize at this \nforecast time. As ingredients are not favored by the latest \nforecast model guidances and environmental conditions for \nstrong storms to develop, we are not expecting any severe \nstorms for tomorrow. For locations north of Albany, a dreary\nTuesday is in store for tomorrow. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nAfternoon Update: No significant changes to the current \nforecast for Friday into this weekend, confidence continues to \nincrease that it's going to be chilly and windy. Read previous \ndiscussion below for more details. \n\nPrevious Discussion: Another frontal boundary looks to cross \nthe region for Friday into Friday night. A strong upper level \ntrough will be passing over the Northeast for the weekend, with \n850 hpa temps dropping down to around -8 to -10 C according to \nthe 00z GEFS, which is well below normal for mid October. 00z \nGEFS 500 hpa heights are about 2-3 STD below normal as well. \nAlthough moisture will be limited behind the cold front, some \nlake enhanced and upslope precip may occur behind the front, \nallowing for some snow showers for western, northern and high \nterrain areas. At this point, its hard to stay how much (if at \nall) any accumulation would occur, but the first flakes of the \nseason are a possibility of the higher terrain areas with the \nexpected chilly air in place. In addition, a strong pressure \ngradient will result in breezy to windy conditions, making it \nfeel even colder through the weekend. Although the growing \nseason has ended for the entire region, a widespread freeze is \nlikely Saturday night for the entire area as well.",
